    "closing": "Server-side apply moves the merge into the API server and keeps a ledger of who owns what in metadata.managedFields. Three things follow, and this question grades all three. First, only an apply patch writes operation: Apply, so kubectl apply --server-side --field-manager=hire is the only route to the required entry; scale, edit, patch and client-side apply all write Update under a manager you cannot choose. Second, an apply owns exactly the fields it sends, which is why the file must contain identity plus replicas plus the annotation and nothing more — send the template and you take it from ops, whose retained ownership is worth two points. Third, a conflict is not a failure, it is the server naming the current owner and offering three resolutions: force and take the field, drop the field and give up the change, or match the value and co-own. The task says transfer, so --force-conflicts is the answer, and afterwards ops correctly no longer lists f:spec/f:replicas while keeping everything else. Read the result with kubectl get -o yaml --show-managed-fields, because kubectl hides the ledger by default. See kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/server-side-apply/."
